WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.313
IaC automation often depends on modules,
providers, plugins, templates,

2
00:00:07.393 --> 00:00:10.421
examples, and shared libraries.

3
00:00:10.501 --> 00:00:15.475
Reuse improves consistency and reduces
duplication,

4
00:00:15.555 --> 00:00:20.529
but it also introduces supply-chain trust
decisions.

5
00:00:20.609 --> 00:00:26.233
A reusable component can create resources,
set permissions,

6
00:00:26.313 --> 00:00:33.233
define network exposure, configure
logging, change encryption defaults,

7
00:00:33.313 --> 00:00:36.774
or alter naming and tagging behavior.

8
00:00:36.854 --> 00:00:43.883
Providers and plugins deserve special
attention because they may run inside

9
00:00:43.963 --> 00:00:49.802
automation environments with access to
credentials and state.

10
00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:56.587
A provider update can change behavior in
ways that matter for security and

11
00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:57.964
reliability.

12
00:00:58.044 --> 00:01:03.776
A module update can introduce new
resources, loosen defaults,

13
00:01:03.856 --> 00:01:07.208
or alter dependency relationships.

14
00:01:07.288 --> 00:01:13.019
These changes should be controlled,
reviewed, and traceable.

15
00:01:13.099 --> 00:01:20.777
Secure teams review module ownership,
provenance, versioning, update practices,

16
00:01:20.857 --> 00:01:23.020
and expected behavior.

17
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:29.696
Version pinning and controlled updates
make changes more predictable.

18
00:01:29.776 --> 00:01:33.886
Pinning is not a substitute for
maintenance,

19
00:01:33.966 --> 00:01:41.103
but it helps prevent an unreviewed
dependency change from becoming a surprise

20
00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:43.021
production change.

21
00:01:43.101 --> 00:01:46.670
Updates should be deliberate, tested,

22
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.292
and reviewed like other infrastructure
changes.

23
00:01:51.372 --> 00:01:58.184
Shared modules should have maintainers,
review history, documentation,

24
00:01:58.264 --> 00:02:00.860
and security expectations.

25
00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:07.104
Defaults should be examined for identity
scope, network exposure,

26
00:02:07.184 --> 00:02:12.915
storage access, logging, encryption, and
deletion behavior.

27
00:02:12.995 --> 00:02:20.132
A module that makes secure behavior easy
and risky behavior obvious is a strong

28
00:02:20.212 --> 00:02:21.293
guardrail.

29
00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:29.051
A module that hides broad access behind
convenience deserves careful review before

30
00:02:29.131 --> 00:02:29.780
reuse.
